WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.327
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:09.280
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:15.994
Unit fifteen: Development Economics. Today you will discuss poverty using break / escape + noun.

4
00:00:16.294 --> 00:00:19.063
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.279
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.117 --> 00:00:25.559
Economic development.

7
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:31.127
Economic development means sustained improvement in incomes and living standards.

8
00:00:31.717 --> 00:00:34.517
Economic development reduced poverty in the region.

9
00:00:35.117 --> 00:00:38.210
Your turn. Say: Economic development.

10
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:42.567
Economic development.

11
00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:44.492
Poverty trap.

12
00:00:45.483 --> 00:00:49.480
Poverty trap means a self-reinforcing cycle that keeps people poor.

13
00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:53.209
A poverty trap forms when low income prevents investment.

14
00:00:53.817 --> 00:00:56.701
Your turn. Say: Poverty trap.

15
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:00.707
Poverty trap.

16
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:02.637
Human capital.

17
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:07.983
Human capital means the skills, knowledge and health of a population.

18
00:01:08.583 --> 00:01:11.814
Investing in human capital raises productivity.

19
00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:15.255
Your turn. Say: Human capital.

20
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.415
Human capital.

21
00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:21.047
Foreign aid.

22
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:26.116
Foreign aid means resources given by one country to another for development.

23
00:01:26.717 --> 00:01:28.275
Foreign aid built the clinic.

24
00:01:28.883 --> 00:01:31.721
Your turn. Say: Foreign aid.

25
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:35.603
Foreign aid.

26
00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.199
Structural transformation.

27
00:01:39.183 --> 00:01:44.190
Structural transformation means the shift of labour from farming to industry and services.

28
00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:48.304
Structural transformation lifted millions into better jobs.

29
00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:52.486
Your turn. Say: Structural transformation.

30
00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:57.577
Structural transformation.

31
00:01:58.317 --> 00:01:59.620
Institutions.

32
00:02:00.617 --> 00:02:05.147
Institutions means the rules and organisations that shape economic behaviour.

33
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.841
Strong institutions protect property and contracts.

34
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:12.160
Your turn. Say: Institutions.

35
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.315
Institutions.

36
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.225
Inequality.

37
00:02:19.217 --> 00:02:23.863
Inequality means unequal distribution of income, wealth or opportunity.

38
00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.380
Inequality can slow growth.

39
00:02:26.983 --> 00:02:29.311
Your turn. Say: Inequality.

40
00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:33.272
Inequality.

41
00:02:34.017 --> 00:02:35.378
Sustainable growth.

42
00:02:36.383 --> 00:02:40.357
Sustainable growth means growth that does not deplete resources for the future.

43
00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.971
Sustainable growth balances income with the environment.

44
00:02:44.583 --> 00:02:47.387
Your turn. Say: Sustainable growth.

45
00:02:50.233 --> 00:02:51.943
Sustainable growth.

46
00:02:52.733 --> 00:02:56.396
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:01.978
Visal, why do some villages stay poor for generations even when people work hard?

48
00:03:03.083 --> 00:03:10.020
Often they are caught in a poverty trap. Low income prevents investment, which keeps income low.

49
00:03:11.117 --> 00:03:12.315
How can they get out?

50
00:03:13.417 --> 00:03:21.874
Investment in education can help break the poverty trap. Health and credit also help families escape the cycle.

51
00:03:22.983 --> 00:03:24.542
Is foreign aid the answer?

52
00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:35.419
Sometimes. Aid can break the cycle if it builds institutions and human capital, but dependence can prevent communities from escaping it.

53
00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:38.322
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:39.183 --> 00:03:43.133
Grammar focus: Break and escape plus noun (a trap, a cycle).

55
00:03:43.433 --> 00:03:44.667
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.661
Investment in education can help break the poverty trap.

57
00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.235
Families need help to escape the cycle of debt.

58
00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:56.357
Well-designed aid can break the cycle of dependence.

59
00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:07.362
break the cycle / break the poverty trap; escape from a cycle or escape the trap. 'Break' takes a direct object. 'Help' can be followed by a bare infinitive: help break.

60
00:04:08.117 --> 00:04:12.499
Be careful! Don't say: Schooling can break the poverty trap off.

61
00:04:14.433 --> 00:04:16.909
Say: Schooling can break the poverty trap.

62
00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:19.475
break + object, no particle.

63
00:04:20.267 --> 00:04:22.977
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:25.116
Escaping the Trap.

65
00:04:25.416 --> 00:04:29.680
Development economics asks why some countries remain poor and how they can grow.

66
00:04:30.667 --> 00:04:37.066
A poverty trap arises when low income prevents the investment in health, skills and capital that would raise income.

67
00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:44.594
Investment in education can help break the trap, since human capital raises productivity across generations.

68
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:51.268
Foreign aid can break the cycle when it builds institutions, though poorly designed aid may foster dependence.

69
00:04:52.267 --> 00:05:00.884
Structural transformation, the shift from farming to industry and services, has allowed countries such as South Korea to escape poverty within a generation.

70
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.967
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:06.817 --> 00:05:10.140
Investment in education can help blank the poverty trap.

72
00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.780
A, break. B, to broke. C, breaking of.

73
00:05:17.333 --> 00:05:19.194
The answer is A: break.

74
00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:23.557
The skills and health of a population are blank capital.

75
00:05:23.857 --> 00:05:27.429
A, human. B, foreign. C, structural.

76
00:05:30.967 --> 00:05:32.931
The answer is A: human.

77
00:05:34.083 --> 00:05:37.790
Rules and organisations that shape economic behaviour are blank.

78
00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:41.987
A, institutions. B, aid. C, traps.

79
00:05:45.533 --> 00:05:47.893
The answer is A: institutions.

80
00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.794
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:51.094 --> 00:05:56.663
A, Aid can break the cycle.. B, Aid can break off the cycle.. C, Aid can to break the cycle..

82
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.070
The answer is A: Aid can break the cycle..

83
00:06:04.217 --> 00:06:07.679
According to the reading, South Korea escaped poverty through…

84
00:06:07.979 --> 00:06:12.991
A, aid only. B, structural transformation. C, isolation.

85
00:06:16.533 --> 00:06:19.833
The answer is B: structural transformation.

86
00:06:21.033 --> 00:06:24.197
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.594
What do you think is the most effective way to reduce global poverty?

88
00:06:45.383 --> 00:06:46.826
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:47.126 --> 00:07:10.214
I think investing in education and health is the most effective way, because it builds human capital that lasts. Combined with fair institutions and access to credit, it helps families escape the poverty trap by themselves. Aid is useful for emergencies, but long-term reduction comes from jobs, skills and stable institutions.

90
00:07:10.514 --> 00:07:12.119
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:13.017 --> 00:07:18.664
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and break and escape plus noun (a trap, a cycle).

92
00:07:18.964 --> 00:07:24.241
Today's value is global responsibility. Act as someone who belongs to the whole world.

93
00:07:24.541 --> 00:07:27.425
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:27.725 --> 00:07:30.377
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

